g.wasm.md:411:Shared helpers own call binding/return/restore, ownership, primitives, stores,\ndocs/design/yin.vm.debruijn.register.md:1130:H, its scalar bytes, its validator, or its wire protocol. It does not add\ndocs/design/yin.vm.debruijn.register.md:1417:lowering validates binding/format, attached origin, and arity against actual\ndocs/design/yin.vm.debruijn.register.md:1454:fixtures, bindings, and compared fields are listed here):\ndocs/design/yin.vm.debruijn.register.md:1455:- `test/yin/vm/semantic/corpus.cljc` **programs and literal goldens**: these goldens specify syntax/address, not a runnable environment or expected final value for every entry. Supply deterministic `f`/`g`/`h`/`k`/`x`/`y`/`c`/`loop` bindings, stream fixtures, and genuine parked targets for resume cases; otherwise compare the same qualified refusal.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_4","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"sed -n '74,116p' docs/design/datom.world.md; sed -n '14,28p' docs/design/yang.wasm.md; sed -n '390,405p' docs/design/yang.wasm.md; sed -n '545,567p' docs/design/yin.vm.engine.md; python3 - <<'PY'\nfrom pathlib import Path\nimport re\nfor name in ['datom.world','yin.vm.engine','yang.wasm','yin.vm.debruijn.register','yin.vm.rust-kernel']:\n p=Path('docs/design')/(name+'.md');s=p.read_text()\n for link in re.findall(r'\\\\]\\\\((\\\\.[\"'^)]+)'\"\\\\)',s):assert (p.parent/link.split('#')[0]).exists(),(p,link)\n assert not re.search(r'transport binding|binding of (?:the|this) ABI|second[ ,\\\\n-]*(?:process-local[ ,\\\\n-]*)?ABI',s,re.I),p\nprint('PASS: file links and category-wording sweep')\nPY\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_4","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"sed -n '74,116p' docs/design/datom.world.md; sed -n '14,28p' docs/design/yang.wasm.md; sed -n '390,405p' docs/design/yang.wasm.md; sed -n '545,567p' docs/design/yin.vm.engine.md; python3 - <<'PY'\nfrom pathlib import Path\nimport re\nfor name in ['datom.world','yin.vm.engine','yang.wasm','yin.vm.debruijn.register','yin.vm.rust-kernel']:\n p=Path('docs/design')/(name+'.md');s=p.read_text()\n for link in re.findall(r'\\\\]\\\\((\\\\.[\"'^)]+)'\"\\\\)',s):assert (p.parent/link.split('#')[0]).exists(),(p,link)\n assert not re.search(r'transport binding|binding of (?:the|this) ABI|second[ ,\\\\n-]*(?:process-local[ ,\\\\n-]*)?ABI',s,re.I),p\nprint('PASS: file links and category-wording sweep')\nPY\"","aggregated_output":"**The system ABI.** One abstraction boundary (`dao.stream`), many\nimplementations (ring-buffer, websocket, UDP, wasm imports). `dao.stream` is\nthe contract for datom.world and `yin.vm`: operations, exhaustive outcome maps,\ncanonical values, append-only batch semantics and observer discipline. It is\nnot a transport implementation or a wire protocol. The wasm import-surface\nimplementation is specified once in [yin.vm.engine.md](./yin.vm.engine.md) §E.3;\nits canonical Jing CBOR representation does not define the abstraction itself.\n\n**Owner clarification, 2026-10-19 (verbatim):** “dao.stream is an abstraction\nboundary. It is not a transport implementation. It can be implemented using a\nring-buffer, a websocket or UDP”\n\nThe wasm import surface is another implementation: adapters implement the\nabstraction through imported functions over linear memory. A co-resident\nproducer can instead use an in-memory ring-buffer implementation inside the\ninstance with zero host crossings. Both preserve the same abstraction contract.\n\nWhat crosses a trust domain is a strict subset of in-machine values: closures\nand continuations cross only as admissible portable plain data; host types,\nresources and code artifacts never do. The custody gates retain this distinction\n([yin.vm.linker.md](./yin.vm.linker.md)); an implementation grants no additional\nportability or authority.\n\n**Owner ruling, strengthened 2026-10-19: the boundary is drawn, not found;\none abstraction boundary is the principle.** The boundary between wasm and datom.world is\n`dao.stream`; its implementation uses whatever wasm exposes to the outside\nworld. The exposed functions are **adapters to dao.stream**, which is the ABI\nto `yin.vm` and the whole ecosystem. Machine-internal work executes inside the\nwasm instance: the Rust engine crates and compiled register bodies share memory\nand direct calls. Only effects/facts cross through the stream adapters. World\nfacts still require interaction with the world; moving engine implementation\ninside the machine does not manufacture those facts.\n\n**Dated supersession (owner boundary clarification, 2026-10-19):** This replaces\nthe earlier same-day “sole justification … performance” allowance for\nprocess-local services and the service-protocol interpretation of\n`yin.vm.engine.host.v1` (historically renamed from `yin.register.host.v1`). Its private operation vocabulary, TLV codec,\nexpanded-canonical-bytes comparator and service-status protocol are dissolved.\nPerformance work optimizes internal execution or the stream implementation; it does\nnot reintroduce a semantic service interface. [yang.wasm.md](./yang.wasm.md)\nis the compiler implementation plan for this in-instance architecture.\n\n**Effects out.** Portable code appends an effect value describing what must\ncompiler implementation plan for register bytecode → wasm. The earlier\nbody-lane-first architecture and `yin.vm.engine.host.v1` service target\n(historically renamed from `yin.register.host.v1`) are superseded. Compile the\nshared Rust engine crates into the instance; imports are only the dao.stream\nimplementation specified in [yin.vm.engine.md](./yin.vm.engine.md) §E.3. Its former\noperation vocabulary, TLV codec/comparator, service statuses and response-refetch\nmachinery are deleted. Engine-in-guest is mandatory, not a W6 experiment. Owner taxonomy clarification,\n2026-10-19: dao.stream is the abstraction boundary; ring-buffer, websocket, UDP\nand the wasm import surface are implementations. The module imports the\nimplementation specified in engine §E.3; a co-resident in-instance ring-buffer\ncan satisfy stream operations without host crossings.\n\nThe 2026-10-19 decomposition remains: engine representation/implementation/conformance\nmethod live in [yin.vm.engine.md](./yin.vm.engine.md) §E; register state,\nfixtures and acceptance gates in [yin.vm.debruijn.register.md](./yin.vm.debruijn.register.md)\n---\n\n## 4. Target linkage: in-instance engine and dao.stream implementation\n\nThe compiler links `yin-value`, `yin-scalar`, `yin-image`, `yin-kernel`,\n`yin-prims`, `yin-engine` and `yin-wire` for `wasm32-unknown-unknown`. The\nimport-surface implementation's operations, signatures, Jing payloads and stream outcomes have one\nhome: [yin.vm.engine.md](./yin.vm.engine.md) §E.3. The compiler emits no private\nservice protocol, codec, transport-status decoder or response-refetch loop.\nThe owner boundary clarification of 2026-10-19 supersedes the former target\ninterface and its earlier performance-based exception.\n\nCompiled bodies and engine functions reach each other by **direct calls and\nshared linear memory**, internal linkage within the task's instance group.\nThe shared helper thunk is the one replaceable generated call site. It flushes\nthe eight register fields, calls the owning crate's transition, and adopts the\n### E.3 The wasm implementation of the dao.stream abstraction\n\n**Owner supersession, 2026-10-19 (boundary clarification):** the former\n`yin.vm.engine.host.v1` service protocol (previous name `yin.register.host.v1`)\nis dissolved, including control-block tables, operation envelope